\title{A stop kept inside UBI that watches the bid or the offer, with an optional real stop behind it}
\description{
Set \code{transaction_type} to the side that opened the position, so a long position is protected by asking for \code{buy}. The stop watches the bid when protecting a long and the offer when protecting a short, so a single stray trade does not fire it. It protects nothing while UBI is down, which is what the backstop is for: a real stop-loss limit placed at the broker at once. With a backstop the answer is HTTP 200 with an \code{outcome} of \code{accepted} and a \code{legs} list holding the backstop at the path \code{root.children.1} with its \code{order_id}; once any of the backstop fills, UBI's own stop stops watching. While the side of the book it watches is empty it does not fire, even if the last trade is past the level, and it sends the template's \code{quantity} rather than sizing itself from the position. \code{trigger_price} here is the hidden level, not the order's own trigger. Without a backstop it answers HTTP 202 with an \code{outcome} of \code{armed} and sends nothing to a broker until it fires, so keep the \code{parent_id} from the answer.

The order template's attributes are described on \code{SyntheticOrder}.
